# === SRS Media Server ===
# Copy to engines/srs/.env — loaded automatically when ENGINE=srs.
# Profile deploys use engines/srs/.env.<profile> (deploy.sh creates it from
# engines/srs/.env on first deploy).

# Segment length and the playlist window, both in seconds. HLS_FRAGMENT has no default and is set in
# the root .env (2 in its sample), because SRS and the uploader both read it. HLS_WINDOW is left unset
# here so a fresh install takes the compose default of 15. Keep the publisher's GOP at or below
# HLS_FRAGMENT, because SRS can only cut on a keyframe and will otherwise emit segments longer than
# this asks for. HLS_WINDOW is a duration, not a count, so keep the two proportional.
#
# The player's DEFAULT_HLS_TUNING.liveSyncDuration (6s) is a latency target clamped to the
# playlist window, so it has to stay comfortably inside HLS_WINDOW. Because both are in
# seconds, that stays true as HLS_FRAGMENT moves.

# === ABR ladder ===
# Off by default: SRS then produces the single rendition it always has. Enabled, the ingest
# vhost stops segmenting and becomes a transcode source, and each rung is republished onto
# ABR_VHOST — a vhost with no transcode block, which is what stops a rung from being
# transcoded again and again. See engines/README.md for the full picture.
#
# ABR_ENABLED, ABR_LADDER and ABR_VHOST are read by the stream-uploader as well as by SRS. SRS's
# webhooks carry a stream name and nothing else, so the geometry and the encoder's target bitrate
# that the master playlist needs are only knowable from this file.
ABR_ENABLED=false

# name:width:height:kbps, space separated. The name becomes the stream-name suffix
# (livestream -> livestream_720p) and is what the uploader groups the ladder by, so keep the
# <height>p form. Widths and heights must be even; H.264 rejects odd ones.
ABR_LADDER=1080p:1920:1080:5000 720p:1280:720:2800 480p:854:480:1200 360p:640:360:700

# ABR_FPS x HLS_FRAGMENT must be a whole number of frames — that product is the GOP, and every
# rung has to place its keyframes at the same media timestamps or a switch lands mid-GOP.
# The entrypoint refuses to start rather than round it. 30 x 0.5 = 15; 25 x 0.5 = 12.5 is not.
ABR_FPS=30
ABR_PRESET=veryfast
# Seconds of VBV buffer, as a multiple of each rung's bitrate. The kbps in ABR_LADDER reaches x264
# as -b:v, an average it is free to overshoot — measured against a 700kbps rung it ran 40% over,
# and every one of those bytes is a chunk a viewer has to retrieve. maxrate plus bufsize makes the
# target a ceiling. 1 is tight, which is what a live ladder wants: a larger buffer lets a complex
# scene borrow bitrate from its neighbours and produces exactly the segment-size spikes that stall
# retrieval. Raise it if quality suffers more than the spikes cost.
ABR_VBV_SECONDS=1
# Seconds SRS keeps the rung encoders after the broadcaster drops. A broadcaster back within it keeps
# the same encoders and its picture returns in about 2 seconds. Keep it short: SRS cuts the idle rung
# publishes 13 to 17 seconds into a drop, and a held encoder whose publish was cut only fails and
# restarts, slower than a fresh set. How long a broadcaster may be away is ORPHAN_REAP_MS in the
# uploader, not this. 0 turns the hold off: SRS then stops the encoders one after another, as stock SRS
# does, and refuses a reconnect until they have stopped. Keep it above 0 while either takeover is on.
ABR_UNPUBLISH_HOLD=12
# Seconds a rung encoder's input or output may stall before ffmpeg exits and SRS starts it again.
# Unset it is ABR_UNPUBLISH_HOLD plus 8, 20 at the default hold, and never below 18. It must be longer than the larger of
# the hold plus 3 seconds and 17 seconds, because a held encoder reads nothing while the broadcaster
# is away and SRS cuts an idle rung publish about 16 seconds after the last packet. The entrypoint
# refuses to start otherwise.
# ABR_IO_TIMEOUT=20
ABR_PROFILE=main
# 0 lets ffmpeg pick. There is one ffmpeg process per rung, each decoding the source
# independently, so four rungs is four decodes — usually the ladder's real CPU floor.
ABR_THREADS=0

# "copy" keeps the publisher's AAC track bit-identical across all four rungs, which costs no CPU
# and removes cross-rung A/V drift. Switch to "aac" if the publisher sends something else.
# Audio is muxed into every rung for the POC rather than split into its own rendition group.
ABR_ACODEC=copy
ABR_AUDIO_BITRATE=128

# The vhost the rungs are republished onto. The RTMP port the republish dials defaults to SRS's own
# RTMP listener (SRS_RTMP_PORT), so it follows --portSlot on its own. Set ABR_RTMP_PORT only to
# override that. Deliberately not SRS's [port] variable: [port] resolves to the port the source
# arrived on, so a source that came in over SRT on 10080 would have its republish aimed at the SRT
# listener (ossrs/srs#4496).
ABR_VHOST=abr
# ABR_RTMP_PORT=

SRS_SRT_PORT=10080
SRT_PASSPHRASE=

# The host addresses SRS's two ingest ports are published on. Empty is every address, because a
# broadcaster dials them from wherever they are, and which of them the internet reaches is the host's
# firewall. They have no effect with host networking (`pnpm srs:host`).
# SRS_SRT_BIND=
# SRS_RTMP_BIND=

# Which host addresses expose SRS's unauthenticated control API and HLS file server. Blank is the
# host's Docker bridge address where the deploy reads it into DOCKER_BRIDGE_ADDRESS, and 127.0.0.1 on
# this engine-only path, where nothing reads it. Docker publishes past a host firewall, so these stay
# off the network unless you name an address, 0.0.0.0 for every one.
# These control Docker-published ports only and have no effect with host networking (`pnpm srs:host`).
SRS_HTTP_API_BIND=
SRS_HTTP_BIND=

# How long SRT holds a packet waiting for a retransmission before delivering without it, in
# milliseconds. Every packet waits this long, so it is also a delay floor on the ingest hop. Too low
# and `tlpktdrop` discards resends that were on their way, and each one becomes a hole in the
# picture. 2000 since 2026-09-23: before that SRS waited 120, because it defaults `recvlatency` to
# 120 and sets it after `latency`, and an ordinary uplink losing 5 to 8.5% had nearly every resend
# dropped as too late. The value now fills both. Lower it only for a publisher on the same host or
# network as the engine. The entrypoint refuses a value that is not a number.
# SRT_LATENCY=2000

# Whether a reconnecting SRT broadcaster replaces a publisher SRS still holds, such as an encoder whose
# network died without closing the connection. Off, SRS refuses the reconnect as busy for about 7 seconds,
# until it notices the dead peer. It is only safe where the uploader refuses a wrong publish key, so left
# unset it is on when PUBLISH_KEY_SECRET or ADMIN_API_URL is set where compose reads its variables, and off
# otherwise. This file is the standalone engine's and carries neither, so here it stays off unless you set
# it. on or off decides it outright, and the entrypoint refuses anything else.
# SRT_TAKEOVER=

# The same for a reconnecting RTMP broadcaster. Off, SRS refuses the reconnect as busy until its own publish
# timeout drops the silent publisher. Left unset it follows the same rule as SRT_TAKEOVER, and on or off decides
# it outright. It applies to broadcasters only: the ABR ladder's rung republishes never get it.
# RTMP is not encrypted. The stream key crosses the network readable, and there is no passphrase as SRT has.
# Anyone who reads a key off the network can publish to that stream, and while this is on they can also take
# a live broadcast over. An SRT passphrase keeps the picture private but not the key: SRT sends its stream id,
# key included, before encryption starts, so a key read off an SRT connection publishes over RTMP too. See the takeover section of engines/README.md.
# RTMP_TAKEOVER=

# Who may play a stream out of SRS, over RTMP and SRT and for a playlist from the file server. RTMP
# publishing and playback share one port, so a firewall cannot let broadcasters in and keep players out,
# and this is what does. Empty is this container alone, where the ABR ladder's transcode input plays
# from, because a viewer reads the broadcast from Swarm. Addresses and CIDR blocks, separated by spaces
# or commas, or all. Publish stays open to every address, because the publish hook checks the key.
# SRS_PLAY_FROM=

# Shared secret SRS carries in its webhook URL. Required, with no unauthenticated mode: these
# webhooks reach the same stamp-spending path as the /stream routes, so an open one is a funds
# drain. SRS cannot sign its callbacks or send a header, so the URL is the only channel it offers.
# Unreserved URL characters only, minimum 32. Generate one with:  openssl rand -hex 32
SRS_WEBHOOK_TOKEN=

# Webhook target — auto-resolved by deploy.sh for Docker deployment.
# Set manually when running SRS standalone (engines/srs/docker-compose.yml).
SRS_ADAPTER_HOST=localhost
SRS_ADAPTER_PORT=3000


# Where the stream-uploader reads HLS segments written by SRS (local dev).
SRS_MEDIA_PATH=../../engines/srs/media

# BEE_URL is a core uploader setting — set it in the root .env / .env.<profile>.
# In deploy.sh it is auto-resolved from config.json unless explicitly overridden.
